WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.304 --> 00:00:07.970
CHRIS LATIMER: My
name is Chris Latimer.

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:11.440
I'm one of the product managers
that works on APIs and API

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.820
management for Google.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.340
So I'm part of the team that
runs the Apigee Platform.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:19.910
And I've been working on
APIs for a really long time.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:23.800
In fact, when I first
started off looking at APIs,

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.434
it was in the context of
service-oriented architecture.

00:00:26.434 --> 00:00:28.600
And we were always having
conversations about things

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:29.950
like, should we use SOAP?

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:30.850
Should we use REST?

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:33.910
Which one is better
for enterprises?

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.490
And at the time,
the feeling was SOAP

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.860
was this enterprise
technology, and REST

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.200
was more something for
the Facebooks and Twitters

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.840
of the world, this kind
of novel technology

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.270
that really wasn't
enterprise-ready.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.060
And so it's interesting to see
the changes that have happened

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:50.470
over the last 10 or so years.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:54.880
Because now APIs are everywhere.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.677
You can't really go more
than 5 or 10 minutes

00:00:57.677 --> 00:01:00.010
over the course of a day
without using some type of API.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:02.170
Any time you pick
up your phone, it's

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:03.770
probably making an API call.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:08.670
So if you check your social
media today, API calls.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.080
If you are checking in for
your flight to go home,

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.152
if you're not a local,
that's probably an API call.

00:01:14.152 --> 00:01:16.960
If you do things like purchase
something online, again,

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.092
another API call.

00:01:18.092 --> 00:01:20.050
And so it should come as
absolutely no surprise

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.000
that, with all of these
critical things that

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.070
are running on APIs
for our organizations,

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:27.300
APIs need security.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.180
And normally, the type of
picture that we think of

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.830
is something like this.

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:35.610
So we don't want to
have our organization be

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.859
on the front page of
Google News saying,

00:01:37.859 --> 00:01:39.400
yet another data
breach has happened.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.608
And now we're responsible
for taking this information

00:01:41.608 --> 00:01:44.100
that our customers
have trusted us with

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.830
and accidentally leaked it out
so that hackers now have access

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.050
to our passwords and
our personal information

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:50.730
and those type of things.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:54.210
And the natural inclination
that we typically have

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:58.110
is to do something like this--
to lock up all of our data.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.059
How can we take our
data and our APIs

00:02:00.059 --> 00:02:02.100
and make them as secure
as possible so absolutely

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.800
nobody can get access to them?

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.210
But the problem is, if that's
our approach to API security

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.390
and, all of a sudden, we've
lock things down so much

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.970
that we can't actually
use them, we're

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.790
going to miss out on
all the value of APIs.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:21.360
And so when we're trying
to actually build out

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.820
API security, locking things
down isn't the hard part.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.640
It would be easy enough for
us just to not build an API.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.010
And that's the most
secure API of all.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.830
But trying to foster
innovation, allowing

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.110
the people who can build
really interesting things

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:38.080
with our APIs,
and not putting in

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.300
place really
heavy-weight bottlenecks

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.080
and unnecessary delays while
keeping our APIs secure

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:47.020
is a much, much harder
problem for us to solve.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.810
And that's what we're going
to talk about here today

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.180
in this session.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:58.420
So the way that we put
this talk together is I

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:00.539
went and I spent a lot
of time-- so in my role,

00:03:00.539 --> 00:03:02.080
I spend a lot of
time with customers.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.690
And I get a chance to
talk with organizations

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.120
from all around the country,
all around the world.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.700
And I like to ask them
what sort of things

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:10.900
they're concerned about.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:12.670
And so what we did
is we started off

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:15.850
by looking at specific
customer examples

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:18.100
and how those customers are
addressing their security

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.320
needs.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.590
So we'll start off with
a pretty basic one.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.390
So we have a lot
of retail customers

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.940
that we work with at Apigee.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.010
And a lot of times, they'll
have APIs like this.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.850
These are things like
store locations and hours.

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:35.300
So we're not looking at
sensitive information.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.821
I mean, we want people to
know when our stores are open.

00:03:37.821 --> 00:03:40.320
We want people to be able to
find our store location so they

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.778
can come to the
stores when they're

00:03:41.778 --> 00:03:43.450
open to do purchasing with us.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.790
But at the same time, it's
important information.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.400
So we probably have
these type of APIs

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.600
attached to our mobile app.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:58.850
We do want to make sure that
this API does remain secure

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:02.646
so that people can
actually use it.

00:04:02.646 --> 00:04:04.020
So what type of
things do we have

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:09.110
to be concerned about with
an API that's like this--

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:13.870
no sensitive information but,
yet, relatively important?

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:15.780
Well, I know that
in a security talk--

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:19.345
I've done a number of security
talks over the years--

00:04:19.345 --> 00:04:20.970
and one of the things
that I've noticed

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:24.497
is that, if I don't mention
TLS as the very first thing,

00:04:24.497 --> 00:04:26.580
and I've got some security
people in the audience,

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.191
you get very nervous that
I didn't mention TLS.

00:04:29.191 --> 00:04:30.690
So I'm going to go
ahead and get out

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.962
of the way at the beginning.

00:04:32.962 --> 00:04:34.920
There are some basic
things that we want to do.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.750
At this point, TLS is
just sort of implied

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:41.200
that we're always going to
put TLS on top of our APIs.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.100
So now that that's out of
the way, the other things we

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.470
have to think about--

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:49.030
one of the one customer that
I talked with, he put it best.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:53.040
He said, "Every time I
onboard a new application,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.560
I've just onboard a
new potential source

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.165
of a DDoS attack."

00:04:57.165 --> 00:04:59.040
And that doesn't
necessarily mean that people

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.167
are intentionally DDoS-ing.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:03.000
But somebody could do
something incorrectly.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.160
They could build an app
that, all of a sudden,

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:07.634
just has more traffic than
they were anticipating.

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:09.300
And all of a sudden,
before you know it,

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.090
you have floods of traffic
that are coming into this API,

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:15.680
and they end up
inadvertently taking it down.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.030
And again, with something
like a store locator,

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:20.250
that means that, if somebody
is on your mobile app,

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:22.166
and they're trying to
get access to your store

00:05:22.166 --> 00:05:24.420
or find the closest store,
suddenly that customer

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.370
can no longer actually
come into the store.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:29.970
So we have to think about
things like, even with this

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.890
API that we want people
to have access to,

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:33.690
we want them to
be able to use it,

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:36.570
how can we allow them to use it
in a way that doesn't introduce

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.510
unnecessary risk?

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:41.910
So traditionally, our approach
to this type of problem

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.220
was to enforce some type
of heavyweight governance.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.487
So before I joined
Apigee, and before we

00:05:48.487 --> 00:05:50.070
were acquired by
Google, I spent a lot

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.780
of time working for a
large insurance company.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.440
In our approach, whenever
we wanted to share data,

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.650
it was very much a matter
of picking up the phone

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:01.420
or sending an email and saying,
hey, I know you have a service.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:03.840
I'd like to use this
in my application.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.690
And usually, the response
was something like, great,

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:07.410
let's sit down.

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:09.330
When can we get together,
and you can fill out

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:11.400
this 100-question questionnaire
about everything you're

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:12.630
going to do with my data.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:14.880
And then we'll understand
your business justification.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.220
And then we'll decide if
we're going to give you access

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:17.820
or not.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:21.630
And over the course of weeks of
getting together and reviewing

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.560
and security
reviews, ultimately,

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:25.320
you would get access to the API.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:28.530
And for no very good
reason, we ended up

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.530
delaying our ability to actually
get something out the door.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:34.170
So a much better
approach this is just

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:35.439
to enforce traffic management.

00:06:35.439 --> 00:06:37.480
There's nothing sensitive
about this information.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.267
It's just store
locations and hours.

00:06:40.267 --> 00:06:42.600
So what if we could give
access to anyone who wanted it,

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.800
either within our
organization or outside,

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.950
depending on the API
you're working with,

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.284
and say, you can have
access to it automatically.

00:06:49.284 --> 00:06:50.700
We'll give you
access to this API.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.110
However, we're going
to limit that access.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:56.730
So if you wanted to make more
than, say, 100 calls an hour

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:59.100
or 1,000 calls an hour,
whatever is appropriate,

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.750
we're just going to start
throttling that traffic,

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:01.870
and we're not going
to give you access.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:04.230
So we don't have to worry
about the flood of traffic

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:07.570
coming in and inadvertently
doing a denial of service.

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:11.690
And we can also open up this
API so more people can use it.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:13.690
The other traditional
approach that we would see

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.231
is something like
a service registry.

00:07:15.231 --> 00:07:17.630
And in this sense, I'm not
talking about the service

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:19.580
registries that have
come along recently

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:21.200
in a microservices-based
architecture.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:25.940
I'm talking about the old-school
web service repositories

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:28.940
and registries and
those type of things

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:30.710
where we would try
to keep track of, OK,

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:32.210
who has access to this API.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:37.040
And we'll note down who might be
the potential source of floods

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.080
of traffic that are coming in.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:40.950
A much better approach
is to automate that.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:44.420
So with platforms like the API
management platforms, usually,

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:46.584
we can do things
like issue API keys.

00:07:46.584 --> 00:07:48.500
And so if we wanted to
be able to look and see

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:51.804
exactly who's making which calls
and how much traffic coming in,

00:07:51.804 --> 00:07:53.720
API keys provide us a
great way of doing that.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:55.094
Now, we have an
identifier we can

00:07:55.094 --> 00:07:57.620
use to track our traffic
that's coming through

00:07:57.620 --> 00:07:59.190
and to enforce those quotas.

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:02.130
So we know that, as an API
call comes in, it has a key,

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:04.430
this is the app, this is
what the apps entitled to.

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:07.877
And we'll start throttling
those things right away.

00:08:07.877 --> 00:08:09.460
The other thing we
have to be aware of

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:10.640
are content-based attacks.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.630
So even though this particular
API doesn't intentionally

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:17.110
expose sensitive data, somebody
may try to get around that.

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:20.152
They may try to send in requests
that the API is not expecting.

00:08:20.152 --> 00:08:22.360
Maybe they're doing things
like SQL injection attacks

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:23.410
or cross-site scripting.

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:24.970
And so we want to
be able to protect

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:28.330
against those
miscreants that are

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:33.645
trying to send in negative
attacks into our API.

00:08:33.645 --> 00:08:35.020
So this is usually
where we start

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:37.270
to think about the
capabilities of an API gateway

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:39.760
and how we can use an
API gateway to intercept

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:42.850
those calls and to actually
enforce rules that will protect

00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:45.070
against content-based attacks.

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:46.780
And even though I
don't want to make

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:49.450
this a commercial about
Apigee, it just so

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:51.280
happens that Google
has a platform called

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:53.510
Apigee that solves all of
these security problems.

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:56.500
And again, if you want to talk
about Apigee, if you have more

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:59.380
questions about your APIs and
API security, I'm happy to dive

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:00.940
into those details.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:03.220
And again, these are
all customer examples

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:05.952
that we've talked to
and worked with as we

00:09:05.952 --> 00:09:06.910
put this talk together.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:14.600
So the next API that we
talked with-- so this

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.690
is actually an
insurance company.

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:21.500
And one of the
challenges with insurance

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:24.270
is that it's a
highly-regulated business.

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:26.880
And so in this
particular example,

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:28.970
what we had is this
insurance company,

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:32.480
they have a product that
they work with a partner.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:35.290
So if you're a Delta Airlines
or a Southwest Airlines,

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:37.587
and you want to offer
travel insurance,

00:09:37.587 --> 00:09:39.170
you can't just offer
your own product.

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:43.280
There are regulations about who
can sell insurance in any given

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:44.150
state.

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:46.640
And so this insurance company
that we were working with

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:48.320
offered a travel
insurance product

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:50.430
that these partners could use.

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.120
And so we were talking with
them about the security concerns

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:55.250
that they had.

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:58.460
So one of the first things
that they were worried about

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.150
is compliance with
industry regulations.

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:02.780
So they basically
said, when we're

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:06.830
working with a Delta Airlines
or some really large company,

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:09.290
they can, of course,
dedicate the six months

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:11.480
required to go through all
of these formal approval

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:12.032
processes.

00:10:12.032 --> 00:10:13.490
We can put in place
the controls we

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:15.770
need to make sure that
we're complying with all

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:17.690
the insurance regulations.

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:20.705
But when we look at the
travel industry as a whole,

00:10:20.705 --> 00:10:22.760
there's a whole niche market.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:27.840
So do we have anybody
here from India?

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:29.460
Seriously?

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:30.480
Nobody at all?

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:31.380
Oh, OK.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:34.510
OK, so I work with I work with
a lot of people from India.

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:38.580
And I've noticed that there are
special dedicated travel sites

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:41.700
that are just for
trips to India,

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:44.460
finding cheap trips to
India, the best times to go,

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:47.730
and they offer ways
to buy this insurance.

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.564
But these are very
small travel sites.

00:10:49.564 --> 00:10:51.105
They're not on par
with a Travelocity

00:10:51.105 --> 00:10:54.155
or an Orbitz or
a Delta Airlines.

00:10:54.155 --> 00:10:55.530
And these smaller
companies, they

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:57.974
can't invest the same
amount of time and effort.

00:10:57.974 --> 00:11:00.390
And there are too many of them
out there for the insurance

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:03.760
company to actually go and
invest that time as well.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:07.290
So they said, we'd like to
reach these smaller companies,

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:11.700
but we're in a quandary
here around exactly how

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:13.272
we're going to do that.

00:11:13.272 --> 00:11:14.730
We have to comply
with regulations.

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:17.790
So how can we help?

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:19.982
So the traditional approach
was we just wouldn't.

00:11:19.982 --> 00:11:21.690
We would just work
with the companies who

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:23.670
could afford to spend
the time with us

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:27.570
to put together a tiger team
that's going to go through.

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:30.660
And six months later of
meetings, and compliance,

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:33.270
and auditors, we'll feel
confident that they can sell

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:37.200
our products, and others can't.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:41.520
So what they found
was they could

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.770
productize APIs in
such a way that they

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:46.750
can offer them in a self-service
way to these smaller companies.

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:48.870
So whereas a large company,
in order to do things

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:53.372
like actually sell
an insurance product,

00:11:53.372 --> 00:11:55.080
you didn't have the
same regulations just

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:57.750
to get a quote on an
insurance product.

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:02.640
So they said, what if we
could take our partner API

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.410
and we could productize that
in such a way that we offer,

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:08.160
in a self-service
automated fashion,

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.527
anybody can come
and offer a quote.

00:12:10.527 --> 00:12:12.360
And we'll do a revenue
share model with them

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:14.049
so that we can comply
with regulations.

00:12:14.049 --> 00:12:15.840
We'll be the ones that
are actually selling

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:17.940
the product, not the partner.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:21.632
But we can offer them a way to
still comply with regulations.

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:26.360
And in doing so, what
they ended up doing was

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:28.400
they would enforce
fine-grained access control.

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:29.960
So depending on
who you are and who

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:32.597
you were that was coming
into their API program,

00:12:32.597 --> 00:12:35.180
they could say, we're only going
to let you see and get access

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:37.730
to these very specific
pieces of our API.

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:40.850
So we can still foster the
innovation that we're after.

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:44.180
But we're only going to give the
full ability to actually sell

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:46.280
an insurance product
to our big partners

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:49.670
who are willing to go
through this process with us.

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:51.740
And when it came to
actually enforcing

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:54.170
this at the runtime
perspective, what they'd do

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:56.730
is they say, well, great,
when somebody signs up,

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:58.230
we're going to give
you credentials.

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:01.700
And you'll use those credentials
to obtain an OAuth token.

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:04.109
So when an OAuth token comes
in, we're going to look,

00:13:04.109 --> 00:13:06.650
and we're going to say, which
products do you have access to?

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:08.024
Do you have access
to the ability

00:13:08.024 --> 00:13:09.860
to sell the insurance
product or just

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:12.200
to get a quote on the
insurance product?

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:13.730
And so in this way,
they were going

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:15.813
to take an existing API
that they had built it out

00:13:15.813 --> 00:13:17.280
for their partners.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.340
And they were able to
repurpose it in such a way

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:23.510
that they can now support a
tremendously larger number

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:26.440
of partners.

00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:30.550
Now, one of the last ones
that we'll talk about here--

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:35.530
so I was working with
a large hospital chain.

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:39.204
And so they probably have one
of the more interesting APIs

00:13:39.204 --> 00:13:40.120
that I've come across.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:46.310
So what they've done is they
have wired up their ICU.

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:49.090
So they have very, very sick
patients that are coming in.

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:51.970
They've got a lot of
sensors and information

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:55.530
that's constantly flowing
back from the ICU.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:58.180
They've got all the
patients' medical data.

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:00.850
And they're taking that,
and they're building out

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:02.100
of a risk assessment.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.494
So every day, if you're a
physician at this hospital

00:14:04.494 --> 00:14:06.910
and you have a patient in the
ICU, it's going to tell you,

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:09.370
here are the patients that are
in the most critical state.

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:11.230
And here are the
things that we've

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:15.940
seen that have led to either
death or negative outcomes

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:19.150
that we should be aware of,
and here is a treatment plan.

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:21.460
And so you can imagine,
in this particular case,

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:24.310
you're dealing with
HIPAA-compliant data.

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:28.480
You're dealing with very
sensitive PII data, of course,

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.170
that's coming through.

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:33.370
And as a result, they had some
really major security concerns.

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:39.190
So what should this
particular organization

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:41.180
be concerned about?

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:43.879
Well, this was, by far and
away, their biggest concern.

00:14:43.879 --> 00:14:45.420
When you're dealing
with HIPAA, there

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:48.910
are real, legal,
criminal consequences

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:50.950
that you can run into
if all of a sudden you

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:57.030
take a patient's
information-- in fact, one

00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:59.950
of the hospitals
that they run, they

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:02.170
ended up having some
celebrities coming in.

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:03.850
And all of a sudden,
you had people

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:08.920
that wanted to take a sneak
peek at their medical records

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:13.010
and ended up finding some
people, actually, in here.

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:15.010
So they wanted to make
sure nobody ended up here

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:17.930
for no good reason.

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:20.460
And so one of the
things I want to make

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:23.310
sure to mention during this talk
is that sometimes this approach

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:24.132
is absolutely OK.

00:15:24.132 --> 00:15:25.590
I mean, there are
going to be times

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:27.900
when we do have incredibly
sensitive information

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:29.530
that we're working with.

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:32.074
And it's going to be
information that absolutely

00:15:32.074 --> 00:15:33.240
does have to be locked down.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:35.070
It's not something
we can take lightly,

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:38.820
that we can allow just
anybody to get access to.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:42.010
And so I don't want you walking
away from this talk thinking,

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:43.800
this guy doesn't
understand our security.

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:45.550
We're in the financial
industry, and we've

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:48.930
got people's bank accounts,
so we need to do this.

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:51.840
Absolutely.

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:53.940
One of the key takeaways
here is that it's really

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:56.700
a matter of looking at the
APIs, understanding the threats,

00:15:56.700 --> 00:16:00.810
understanding how we can get
the API out as much as possible,

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:04.020
and recognizing that sometimes
we don't want it out at all.

00:16:06.920 --> 00:16:09.520
So in this case they
had to figure out,

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:13.340
who's actually authorized
to access patient data.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:17.110
So in this case, it was
very clear that they had--

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:20.346
depending on the patient's you
were assigned as a physician,

00:16:20.346 --> 00:16:21.970
they wanted to make
sure you would only

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:25.510
see the information
for your patients.

00:16:25.510 --> 00:16:29.350
And so as a result, we had to
have a way for these doctors,

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:34.130
and nurses, and other
caregivers to authenticate.

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:37.540
And so traditionally, one of
the problems we've faced--

00:16:37.540 --> 00:16:40.420
and especially in
the web space--

00:16:40.420 --> 00:16:41.860
is direct user authentication.

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:43.450
And we've seen, over
the years, we've

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:45.220
done single
sign-on-type solutions

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:48.640
and things like that, whole
products have sprung up

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:50.920
to solve the SSO problem.

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:53.830
And so from an API
perspective, we

00:16:53.830 --> 00:16:58.210
have a similar construct with
a delegated identity and user

00:16:58.210 --> 00:16:58.840
authorization.

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:00.423
So a lot of times,
this will be things

00:17:00.423 --> 00:17:04.720
like OpenID Connect where we
can allow users to sign in

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:06.369
without ever exposing
our passwords

00:17:06.369 --> 00:17:10.510
and our credentials
to that application.

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:13.117
We can use OpenID Connect
and obtain an OAuth token.

00:17:13.117 --> 00:17:15.700
So this is often what we would
call a three-legged OAuth where

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:16.569
somebody will sign in.

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:18.359
If you've seen things like
sign in with Google or sign

00:17:18.359 --> 00:17:20.319
in with Facebook, a
lot of organizations

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:23.480
are building their own
sign in with our platform

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:26.170
and then using those in
conjunction with API management

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:29.230
to send an OpenID Connect
request, get back the response,

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:31.477
and then exchange that
for an access token.

00:17:31.477 --> 00:17:33.560
And now, what we can do
is we can identify the end

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:35.470
user that's making
these API calls,

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:37.660
and we can do fine-grained
access control

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:40.630
checks to make sure
that only the physicians

00:17:40.630 --> 00:17:43.810
and other caregivers
that should have access

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:48.594
to this particular patient's
information have access.

00:17:48.594 --> 00:17:50.760
One of the other things
that they had to think about

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:53.417
was auditing.

00:17:53.417 --> 00:17:55.500
And so they wanted to be
able to keep track of who

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:57.190
looked at a patient's data.

00:17:57.190 --> 00:18:00.180
So again, when
we're looking at--

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:02.574
not just from the
standpoint of people

00:18:02.574 --> 00:18:03.990
trying to hack
into this account--

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:08.550
but just from an auditing
and compliance requirements

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:13.560
standpoint, a lot of times they
would have specific samplings

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:14.820
that they would have to do.

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:16.440
So they had to go
back and prove--

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:19.590
you've built this API, and over
the course of the last year--

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:23.010
let's pick a specific patient,
anonymized, of course,

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:25.290
and let's look at who
actually made a request in

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:27.927
to get this particular
patient's data.

00:18:27.927 --> 00:18:29.510
And so what they had
to do is they had

00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:31.100
to put in place audit logging.

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:32.850
And so we could go
back, and we could then

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:34.137
look and say, OK, great.

00:18:34.137 --> 00:18:35.970
For this particular
patient, who has looked?

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:38.310
And let's make sure
that they have access

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:41.489
to have looked at that,
they were authorized

00:18:41.489 --> 00:18:42.780
to look at that patient's data.

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:49.830
So even with all of
these capabilities

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:52.520
that we've talked about, we
can still end up with cases

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:56.455
where attackers find a way.

00:18:56.455 --> 00:18:57.830
And so to talk
about that, I want

00:18:57.830 --> 00:19:00.800
to invite up my
colleague Matt Blumberg,

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:04.370
to talk a little bit about
how we would address those.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:05.760
MATT BLUMBERG: Thanks, Chris.

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:07.710
Good morning, everybody.

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:09.110
So my name is Matt Blumberg.

00:19:09.110 --> 00:19:11.870
And I'm a product manager
focused on API security

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:13.520
at Apigee.

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:16.460
And as Chris talked
about, API abuse

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:18.440
is becoming much
more common and is

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:21.380
predicted to be the most common
attack vector in the enterprise

00:19:21.380 --> 00:19:23.310
space in the next few years.

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:25.610
And I'm going to talk
about some of the features

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:29.630
that we offer to help you
protect your APIs from abuse.

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:31.820
So just with a raise of
hands, how many people

00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:36.270
currently expose APIs
through a web app?

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:37.200
About 3/4?

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:40.770
And how many are exposing
APIs through a mobile app?

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:42.400
OK, about the same number.

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:44.200
So over the past
couple of years,

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:47.730
we've seen a significant growth
in public-facing APIs, which

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:50.220
is a very powerful
tool that will help

00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:51.556
you transform your business.

00:19:51.556 --> 00:19:53.430
But it definitely comes
with a responsibility

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:54.930
to protect those APIs.

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:58.080
And as much as the
security tools out there

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:01.560
today are great,
sometimes, abuse

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:03.760
can become much
more of a concern.

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:06.150
So in the enterprise
space, customers typically

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:07.920
take a multilayer approach.

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:10.800
And they typically deploy
a web application firewall.

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:13.620
And that does a relatively
good job for web,

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:16.260
protecting from the DDoS and
SQL injection, these types

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:18.360
of attacks, but
they fail to handle

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:22.890
the API-specific attacks which
is where Apigee Sense comes in.

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:24.900
So Sense leverages
API constructs

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:27.420
such as API keys
and access tokens

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:30.780
in the context of the workflow
to help identify behavior

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:33.610
which is past the WAF layer.

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:36.570
So when we analyze information
such as the user-agent and API

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:38.580
key and look at the
volume of that traffic

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:41.030
with other attributes
over a period of time,

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:44.610
we're able to detect API abuse
and bot traffic that WAFs

00:20:44.610 --> 00:20:47.219
are not able to detect.

00:20:47.219 --> 00:20:49.010
So I'm going to talk
for a minute about how

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:50.750
Apigee Sense works.

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:53.490
So we offer what we call
a closed-loop protection.

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:57.530
So this means that we're able to
analyze all of the API traffic

00:20:57.530 --> 00:20:59.367
as it streams
across your proxies.

00:20:59.367 --> 00:21:00.950
And we'll collect
this data and run it

00:21:00.950 --> 00:21:02.325
through our analysis
engine which

00:21:02.325 --> 00:21:04.400
detects anomalies and abuse.

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:06.110
And then our Sense
Console offers

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:08.480
insights about what type
of traffic it's observing

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:11.379
and allows you to review that
traffic and take action on it,

00:21:11.379 --> 00:21:12.920
including blocking
it or flagging it.

00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:16.260
And we'll talk about
that in a minute.

00:21:16.260 --> 00:21:17.840
So I'm going to walk
you through what

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:21.470
a common attack may look like.

00:21:21.470 --> 00:21:23.399
For many customers,
there's an expectation

00:21:23.399 --> 00:21:25.190
of where they operate
their business, where

00:21:25.190 --> 00:21:26.610
traffic should be coming from.

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:29.090
So maybe you're selling
products online.

00:21:29.090 --> 00:21:31.864
Maybe you're a domestic airline.

00:21:31.864 --> 00:21:34.280
But you typically know where
traffic should be coming from

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:37.100
or, definitely, where it
should not be coming from.

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:38.750
And at a glance
here, you can see

00:21:38.750 --> 00:21:43.240
that the world map is covered
in higher-than-normal traffic

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:44.240
from all over the world.

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:47.026
So many countries,
there may be--

00:21:47.026 --> 00:21:48.650
you're not allowed
to conduct business,

00:21:48.650 --> 00:21:50.580
but you're seeing a
significant amount of traffic.

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:51.996
And in the top
corner, you can see

00:21:51.996 --> 00:21:54.480
that the bot traffic has
increased by nearly 30%

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:55.740
in the near term.

00:21:55.740 --> 00:22:00.690
So clearly, there is an
attack going on in this case.

00:22:00.690 --> 00:22:03.770
So as we dive deeper to
investigate what's going on,

00:22:03.770 --> 00:22:07.100
we can start by reviewing the
API abuse in the Detection

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:08.430
Report tool.

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:12.470
So this is where Sense analyzes
and lets you act on behaviors

00:22:12.470 --> 00:22:16.514
that we can see that
exceed thresholds

00:22:16.514 --> 00:22:17.680
that are set in the product.

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:21.140
So for example, there's
a Brute Guesser rule

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:24.730
which, in this case, was
violated more than 3,500 times,

00:22:24.730 --> 00:22:28.520
3,500 different bots, resulting
in millions of malicious API

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:29.810
calls.

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:32.060
There's a full list of the
rules here that you can see

00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:36.400
and the number of bots that
are exceeding those thresholds.

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:39.350
So this gives you an overview
of the different types

00:22:39.350 --> 00:22:42.119
of protection that are offered
through the Sense product.

00:22:42.119 --> 00:22:43.535
So in the previous
slide, I showed

00:22:43.535 --> 00:22:46.430
an example of the Brute
Guesser policy being violated.

00:22:46.430 --> 00:22:48.320
And you see higher
than normal traffic

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:50.690
that results in errors
over a period of time.

00:22:50.690 --> 00:22:54.300
So that may be someone
trying to attempt to log in.

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:57.170
Or another example is
the Content Scraper rule

00:22:57.170 --> 00:22:59.891
where a very common attack used
to scrape products and pricing

00:22:59.891 --> 00:23:00.390
data.

00:23:00.390 --> 00:23:04.310
So if you have mobile
apps for shopping or web

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:06.290
apps for the same
use case, those

00:23:06.290 --> 00:23:09.340
may be scraped for pricing
product information

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:11.940
or, again, login-APIs and
these types of things.

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:15.050
So we have these various
thresholds and sets

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:18.904
of rules that offer protection
for those types of attacks.

00:23:18.904 --> 00:23:20.320
So if we go back
to the attack, we

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:22.250
can see that all
of the API calls

00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:25.160
were going after the
login-URI endpoint here.

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:28.040
So you can see that
the highest value

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:30.800
attacks, such as purchasing
payments, loyalty programs,

00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:34.070
gift cards, and login are
the most commonly abused

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:36.770
and require the highest
level of protection

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:38.000
using tools such as Sense.

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:42.930
If we drill down on
a specific IP address

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:44.880
to investigate
further, we can also

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:47.540
see that most of the calls--
in this case, all of them--

00:23:47.540 --> 00:23:49.740
were resulting in 401's
and 403's, meaning

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:51.212
unauthorized access.

00:23:51.212 --> 00:23:53.670
So this is what triggered the
rule in this particular case.

00:23:57.236 --> 00:23:59.360
So after review, I think
we have enough information

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:01.160
to make a decision to
block this traffic.

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:02.840
So Apigee Sense
offers you the ability

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:07.040
to allow, block, or
flag the API traffic.

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:10.550
So flagging would allow
you to add custom metadata

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:12.320
to the headers,
which may enable you

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:14.900
to configure a honeypot
or other system to deal

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:16.092
with the traffic.

00:24:16.092 --> 00:24:18.050
You're also allowed to
block it, at this point,

00:24:18.050 --> 00:24:20.270
to prevent that IP from
being able to continue

00:24:20.270 --> 00:24:21.650
making API calls.

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:24.447
Or maybe you're running a flash
sale or some other scenario

00:24:24.447 --> 00:24:26.780
where you explicitly want to
allow this type of traffic,

00:24:26.780 --> 00:24:27.904
but that's in your control.

00:24:30.420 --> 00:24:32.420
So as we can see here,
once we took action,

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:34.026
the malicious traffic stopped.

00:24:34.026 --> 00:24:36.650
And we're constantly working on
adding new methods of detection

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:39.630
across our API products.

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:41.900
Google is making a very
large investment in security,

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:44.810
as you may have seen from
other sessions at Next.

00:24:44.810 --> 00:24:46.970
And in Apigee,
it's no different.

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:50.120
We're making a very large
investment collaborating

00:24:50.120 --> 00:24:52.940
across teams with the
broader Google security teams

00:24:52.940 --> 00:24:55.760
to take the best security
features, machine learning,

00:24:55.760 --> 00:24:57.530
and automation from
Google's products

00:24:57.530 --> 00:25:01.890
and integrate that into the
future Apigee API products

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:04.940
in the security area as
well as other functionality.

